  <div class="slide caption-page" data-document-role="page" data-label="C11 - Caption (copy/paste)">
    <div class="caption-flag">Caption - not for posting</div>
    <div class="caption-body">When your child says a tag hurts, their brain may be registering that touch as a threat, not just an annoyance.<br><br>In tactile over-responsivity, the brain over-attends to light touch, treating a seam, a tag, or a sock line as something to defend against. That can trigger a genuine fight-or-flight response in the body. Their nervous system has already sounded an alarm before they can even think it through.<br><br>That is exactly why 'just ignore it' does not work. You cannot ignore an alarm that has already gone off. The discomfort is not a preference to talk them out of, and it is not exaggeration. It is a real nervous system response. Believe them about the tag.</div>
    <div class="caption-tags">#tactileoverresponsivity #clothingsensitivity #fightorflight</div>
    <div class="caption-accounts">Tag: none needed</div>
