    <div class="caption-body">Sensory needs are not something a child picks up or chooses. They come from how that child's brain is wired to process the world.<br><br>Sensitivity is rooted in real, biologically based differences in neural processing, not in a phase, a habit, or a bid for attention. Which means the goal was never to fix it or wait it out. The goal is to understand it and support it.<br><br>When we work with a child's brain instead of fighting against it, so much gets easier for everyone. Accommodating a sensory need is not spoiling or giving in. It is meeting a real, brain-based need in a real body. That is the whole heart of it. Understood, not changed.</div>
